# A clean binary Markov chain  x0 -> x1 -> x2 -> ... -> x7  (8 atoms).
#
# Every sentence is family-local: a prior on x0 and a transition P(x_{i+1} | x_i)
# (one bound for x_i=1, one for x_i=0) on each link. There is NO cross-family
# sentence, so the LCN's only cross-family constraints are the LMC independence
# assertions (x_{i+1} _||_ {x_0..x_{i-1}} | x_i) -- and those are STRUCTURAL: the
# chain-graph junction tree enforces them through its separators (running
# intersection), so CredalJT (scheme D5) does not impose them and does not let
# them inflate the treewidth.
#
# Treewidth of a chain is 1: CredalJT builds n-1 = 7 clusters {x_i, x_{i+1}} with
# separators {x_i}, and solves an NLP with sum_i 2^|c_i| = 7 * 2^2 = 28 cluster-
# marginal variables -- exponential in the treewidth (2^2), NOT in the number of
# atoms (the full joint is 2^8 = 256). It returns the EXACT chain-graph marginals,
# matching ExactInference's full-joint solve. This is the favourable case for D5
# (local cross-family coupling); contrast examples/alarm.lcn, whose cross-family
# LMC assertions span all five atoms.
P0:  0.3 <= P(x0)        <= 0.5
T0a: 0.2 <= P(x1 | x0)   <= 0.4
T0b: 0.6 <= P(x1 | !x0)  <= 0.8
T1a: 0.2 <= P(x2 | x1)   <= 0.4
T1b: 0.6 <= P(x2 | !x1)  <= 0.8
T2a: 0.2 <= P(x3 | x2)   <= 0.4
T2b: 0.6 <= P(x3 | !x2)  <= 0.8
T3a: 0.2 <= P(x4 | x3)   <= 0.4
T3b: 0.6 <= P(x4 | !x3)  <= 0.8
T4a: 0.2 <= P(x5 | x4)   <= 0.4
T4b: 0.6 <= P(x5 | !x4)  <= 0.8
T5a: 0.2 <= P(x6 | x5)   <= 0.4
T5b: 0.6 <= P(x6 | !x5)  <= 0.8
T6a: 0.2 <= P(x7 | x6)   <= 0.4
T6b: 0.6 <= P(x7 | !x6)  <= 0.8
